+## [Unreleased]
+
+### Added
+
+- **OKF v0.2 base layer** (`checkOkfBaseLayer`, on by default): LOKF Enforcer now also checks the plain-OKF v0.2 rules the LOKF schema already subsumes, so a bundle stays checkable even if no separate OKF validator is installed or maintained. The new `okf/*` findings cover a required `type` (§4.1/§11), the `Attested Computation` contract shape (§10 - `runtime`, and `parameters`/`executor`/`attester`/`computation` shape, plus a body `# Computation` block or a `computation` path), reserved `index.md`/`log.md` structure (§8/§9 - a non-root `index.md` carries no frontmatter; `log.md` date headings are ISO 8601), and v0.1→v0.2 migration hints (§13 - a legacy `timestamp`, a body `# Citations` list). **Severity follows the authoritative spec:** what OKF marks REQUIRED/MUST or lists under §11 conformance is an error (a missing `type`, a missing AC `runtime`, a non-root `index.md` with frontmatter, a non-ISO `log.md` date heading, and - in the §5 trust layer - a `generated` with no `by` or a `sources` entry with no `resource`; all `required` in the LOKF schema too); optional-field shape and the §13 migration hints stay warnings. A new **Enforce OKF conformance as errors** toggle (on by default) lets a vault temporarily downgrade just those OKF-required errors to warnings - kept visible, not blocking - e.g. mid-migration, without touching LOKF's own structural errors. Switchable off entirely in *Settings → OKF v0.2 base layer* for a vault that runs a dedicated OKF validator; the "recommend an OKF validator" notice is now off by default. The note body is threaded to the rule engine for the body-shaped checks, degrading gracefully on the metadata-cache path. Import-free, Node-tested `src/validator.ts`.
+- **Look up a LOKF field** (command): a searchable reference of the LOKF frontmatter fields and what each expects - so a user unsure what a property means can look it up in the editor (Obsidian's Properties widget has no per-property description API). Descriptions come straight from the schema's own slot definitions (via the vocabulary manifest that `build-vocab.mjs` fills from `lokf vocab --all --json`, or `lokf.yaml`): the schema is the single source of truth, and `src/fields.ts` only chooses which fields to surface and in what order, so the wording never forks. A smoke test asserts every surfaced field is a real schema slot and that each description stays short enough for the modal. The `base_iri` guidance (an *identifier namespace that need not resolve* - a [Cool URI](https://www.w3.org/Provider/Style/URI) is the ideal, not a requirement) now lives in the schema itself, so every consumer of the schema shares it. Import-free, Node-tested `src/fields.ts`; the README's `base_iri` rule says the same.
+- **Generate Obsidian affordances**: three commands that project a bundle's LOKF facts into Obsidian-native conventions, so a freshly opened bundle isn't inert. "Add Obsidian affordances to the active note" and "Generate Obsidian affordances across the vault" write each concept's Diátaxis `#genre` tag and its typed relations - as a `## Related` `[[wikilink]]` list - into one managed block, so the tag pane groups the bundle and the graph and backlinks light up; "Generate the Diátaxis map for this bundle" turns the `genre` facet into a `diataxis.md` Map of Content grouped into the four Diátaxis quadrants (a reserved bundle file, never validated or curated as a concept). Marker-delimited and idempotent - a re-run regenerates in place, never appends, and never touches frontmatter or human prose; the vault-wide command is confirmation-gated and previews the exact count. Deliberately does *not* map lifecycle or trust onto Obsidian "maturity" tags (a separate axis). Import-free, Node-tested `src/affordances.ts`.
+
